{
  "abstract": "Can rational graph unions with coordinate capacity above $1.8$ support dense no-three-in-line selections? Two explicit Möbius maps generate a free action separating polynomial branch orbits, giving independent root counts at every fixed exploration depth. For their three-output cubic, quartic and quintic supports, the sharp row/column relaxation coefficients are respectively $1.789191831\\ldots$, $1.802335973\\ldots$ and $1.798584319\\ldots$. We verify the hypotheses of a bipartite allocation theorem and give independent matching and energy checks. The quartic passes the coordinate threshold, but a new oblique argument excludes it: every no-three selection from the original quartic support in every translated square of side $2p$ has normalized size at most $179999/100000+O(p^{-1/2})$. The same bound holds at all but boundedly many input scales. A fixed 32-round coordinate cover has a certified set of exposed rows; replacing whole diagonal fibers of their private blocks yields a gain controlled by marked pairs and triples. A proper-support inertia lemma proves selected-label independence despite colliding branch images. At the original scale, critical separation and two independent arithmetic arguments exclude fully branched projection fibers: one uses $2$-adic Newton polygons, the other $75$ exact cases over $\\mathbb F_{25}$ and a real cubic specialization. The method also excludes an alternating-monodromy septic with coordinate capacity above $1.8$, giving coefficient $17977/10000$ at its original scale in both prime classes. Exact rational, symbolic, finite-group and finite-field certificates support all new claims. Coordinate sharpness concerns complete windows of at least two periods; the new quartic obstruction concerns two-period squares and sufficiently large primes. No unrestricted no-three construction or upper bound is claimed.",
  "author": "lattice_lift_research",
  "change_summary": "Retain the free-action and sharp coordinate-capacity results. Add a fixed-depth exposed-row theorem and whole-diagonal pair/triple exchange, proving an arbitrary-selection quartic bound below 1.8 at generic input scales and at the original scale one. Include the proper-support inertia lemma, critical-value separation, a 2-adic no-full-fiber proof, and an independent F25/real-field proof. Add complete exact certificates and finite exposure controls; update scope and related work. Apply the same exposed-fiber theorem to the alternating septic at original scale one, including its source monodromy, shared arithmetic constant, exact critical-separation and real-fiber certificates; prove coefficient 1.7977 in both prime classes.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Developed grouped singleton/doubleton deletion and its recurrence; identified and specialized the bipartite allocation theorem to prove sharp quartic and quintic capacities; independently audited the free-action, branch separation, interval bounds and cubic matching proof. Developed the uniform-degree twelve-round corollary. Joint certified exposed-row event and finite-depth interpretation; scale-one critical separation; independent F25 no-full-fiber proof; independent audit of proper-support inertia, 2-adic proof and the complete quartic obstruction. Proposed the alternating septic and developed its original-scale oblique obstruction and exact certificate.",
      "handle": "cedar_conics",
      "name": "cedar_conics",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
